from (values
('110.38.c.8.E',4,'A recurring image of rivers in a novel about change is an example of what?','["an oxymoron", "an index", "a motif", "a pun"]'::jsonb,2,'Motifs recur with meaning.','original'),
('110.38.c.8.E',3,'Saying ''Nice weather we''re having'' during a hurricane is what?','["a motif", "a flashback", "an epigraph", "sarcasm or verbal irony"]'::jsonb,3,'The speaker means the opposite.','original'),
('110.38.c.8.F',3,'A narrator''s plain, everyday vocabulary and regional dialect most contribute to what?','["a distinct voice", "a legal tone", "a rhyme scheme", "a mathematical tone"]'::jsonb,0,'Diction shapes voice.','original'),
('110.38.c.8.F',4,'Long, winding sentences with many clauses in a reflective essay tend to produce which mood?','["abrupt", "meditative", "comic", "frantic"]'::jsonb,1,'Syntax slows the pace.','original'),
('110.38.c.8.F',3,'Terse, blunt sentences in a hard-boiled narrative create what tone?','["warm and dreamy", "ornate", "stark and unsentimental", "gently humorous"]'::jsonb,2,'Syntax shapes tone.','original'),
('110.38.c.8.G',3,'''Ask not what your country can do for you; ask what you can do for your country'' is an example of which device?','["onomatopoeia", "alliteration only", "simile", "antithesis"]'::jsonb,3,'Contrasting ideas in parallel form.','original'),
('110.38.c.8.G',3,'Repeating ''I have a dream'' at the start of successive sentences is an example of what?','["anaphora", "antithesis", "irony", "hyperbole"]'::jsonb,0,'Anaphora repeats at the start.','original'),
('110.38.c.8.G',4,'A speaker begins with statistics, then shifts to a personal story. What is the likely purpose of the shift?','["to confuse the audience", "to add emotional appeal", "to avoid the topic", "to end the speech"]'::jsonb,1,'Shifts balance logic and feeling.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.B.i',3,'An essay comparing two poets should likely be organized in what way?','["in random order", "by alphabetical title", "point by point or subject by subject", "as a single sentence"]'::jsonb,2,'Comparison needs a pattern.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.B.ii',3,'Which choice best develops the claim that a setting is oppressive?','["naming the author", "listing the street address only", "saying it is a place", "describing low ceilings, locked windows and constant noise"]'::jsonb,3,'Specific details develop an idea.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.B.ii',4,'What distinguishes depth of thought in an essay?','["explaining why evidence matters, not just presenting it", "repeating the thesis", "adding more paragraphs", "using longer words only"]'::jsonb,0,'Commentary shows thinking.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.C',3,'A writer reorders the middle paragraphs so the strongest point comes last. What aspect is improved?','["spelling", "organization", "typeface", "margins"]'::jsonb,1,'Revision for emphasis and flow.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.C',3,'Replacing ''said'' with ''insisted'' in dialogue improves what?','["page count", "spelling", "diction and precision", "capitalization"]'::jsonb,2,'A stronger verb conveys tone.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.D',3,'Which sentence is correctly punctuated?','["Although, the road was closed we found another route.", "Although the road was closed we found, another route.", "Although the road was closed we, found another route.", "Although the road was closed, we found another route."]'::jsonb,3,'A comma follows an introductory clause.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.D',3,'Which sentence maintains a consistent verb tense?','["He walked to the mill and saw the smoke.", "He walk to the mill and seen the smoke.", "He walks to the mill and saw the smoke.", "He walked to the mill and sees the smoke."]'::jsonb,0,'Both verbs are past tense.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.D',3,'In ''the delegates met in philadelphia in may to write the constitution,'' which words need capital letters?','["the and write only", "the, philadelphia, may and constitution", "delegates and met only", "philadelphia only"]'::jsonb,1,'Capitalize the first word, place names, months and the name of a document like the Constitution.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.D',3,'Which sentence uses a dash correctly to set off a phrase?','["The senator tired—but determined took the—stage.", "The senator tired but—determined took—the stage.", "The senator—tired but determined—took the stage.", "The—senator tired but determined took the stage."]'::jsonb,2,'Dashes enclose the interruption.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.D',3,'Which sentence uses parentheses correctly?','["The festival held (every spring draws) thousands.", "The festival held every spring draws (thousands).", "The (festival held every spring draws thousands.)", "The festival (held every spring) draws thousands."]'::jsonb,3,'Parentheses enclose added information.','original'),
('110.38.c.9.D',3,'Which sentence has no spelling errors?','["The committee will receive the necessary documents.", "The committee will receive the neccessary documents.", "The comittee will recieve the neccessary documents.", "The committee will recieve the necessary documents."]'::jsonb,0,'Common misspellings are fixed.','original'),
('110.38.c.10.D',3,'In a business letter requesting information, where does the writer usually state the purpose?','["in the return address", "in the first paragraph", "in the postscript only", "after the signature"]'::jsonb,1,'Professional letters get to the point.','original'),
('110.38.c.10.D',3,'Which closing is appropriate for a letter to a prospective employer?','["Cheers, buddy,", "Later,", "Respectfully yours,", "See ya,"]'::jsonb,2,'Formal closings fit professional letters.','original'),
('110.38.c.11.E',3,'A student researching the Dust Bowl needs primary sources. Which is a primary source?','["a modern textbook chapter", "an encyclopedia entry", "a movie review", "a farmer''s diary from the 1930s"]'::jsonb,3,'Primary sources come from the time period.','original'),
('110.38.c.11.F',4,'A researcher notices two sources disagree on a number. What should the researcher do when synthesizing?','["explain the discrepancy and favor the better-supported figure", "hide the conflict", "drop both sources", "pick the larger number"]'::jsonb,0,'Synthesis addresses conflicts honestly.','original'),
('110.38.c.11.H',3,'Which is an example of properly quoting a source?','["changing one word and omitting the source", "placing the exact words in quotation marks with a citation", "citing the wrong author", "using the exact words with no marks"]'::jsonb,1,'Direct quotes need marks and credit.','original'),
('110.38.c.11.G.i',3,'Which clue most strongly suggests an article is credible?','["only emotional language", "anonymous claims", "named authors with relevant expertise and cited sources", "no author listed"]'::jsonb,2,'Credible sources show expertise and evidence.','original'),
('110.38.c.11.G.i',4,'An advocacy group''s report mentions only data supporting its goal. What is the concern?','["correct citation format", "too many experts", "too much detail", "possible bias through omission"]'::jsonb,3,'Selective presentation skews conclusions.','original'),
('110.38.c.11.G.ii',3,'''Everyone I know likes this candidate, so the candidate will win'' is an example of what?','["hasty generalization", "an appeal to experts", "a rhyme", "valid reasoning"]'::jsonb,0,'A small sample is overgeneralized.','original'),
('110.38.c.11.G.ii',3,'''If we don''t pass this law, our whole economy will collapse'' offers what kind of reasoning when no evidence is given?','["a statistical proof", "an unsupported either-or claim", "an expert citation", "a balanced analysis"]'::jsonb,1,'It oversimplifies outcomes.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'English Language Arts and Reading' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
